#: ../../source/references_other.md:1376 ../../source/references_other.md:1392
msgid "async_op (bool, optional) – Whether this op should be an async op."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1346 ../../source/references_other.md:1358
#: ../../source/references_other.md:1378 ../../source/references_other.md:1394
msgid ""
"Async work handle, if async_op is set to True. None, if not async_op or "
"if not part of the group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1348
msgid "Reduces, then scatters a tensor to all ranks in a group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1350
msgid ""
"output (Tensor) – Output tensor. It should have the same size across all "
"ranks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1352
msgid ""
"input (Tensor) – Input tensor to be reduced and scattered. Its size "
"should be output tensor size times the world size. The input tensor can "
"have one of the following shapes: (i) a concatenation of the output "
"tensors along the primary dimension, or (ii) a stack of the output "
"tensors along the primary dimension. For definition of “concatenation”, "
"see torch.cat(). For definition of “stack”, see torch.stack()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1360
msgid ""
"Split input tensor and then scatter the split list to all processes in a "
"group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1362
msgid ""
"Later the received tensors are concatenated from all the processes in the"
" group and returned as a single output tensor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1366
msgid "output (Tensor) – Gathered concatenated output tensor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1368
msgid "input (Tensor) – Input tensor to scatter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1370
msgid ""
"output_split_sizes – (list[Int], optional): Output split sizes for dim 0 "
"if specified None or empty, dim 0 of output tensor must divide equally by"
" world_size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1372
msgid ""
"input_split_sizes – (list[Int], optional): Input split sizes for dim 0 if"
" specified None or empty, dim 0 of input tensor must divide equally by "
"world_size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1380
msgid "all_to_all_single is experimental and subject to change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1382
msgid ""
"Scatters list of input tensors to all processes in a group and return "
"gathered list of tensors in output list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1386
msgid ""
"output_tensor_list (list[Tensor]) – List of tensors to be gathered one "
"per rank."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1388
msgid ""
"input_tensor_list (list[Tensor]) – List of tensors to scatter one per "
"rank."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1396
msgid "all_to_all is experimental and subject to change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1398
msgid "Synchronize all processes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1400
msgid ""
"This collective blocks processes until the whole group enters this "
"function, if async_op is False, or if async work handle is called on "
"wait()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1406
msgid ""
"device_ids ([int], optional) – List of device/GPU ids. Only one id is "
"expected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1410
msgid ""
"ProcessGroupNCCL now blocks the cpu thread till the completion of the "
"barrier collective."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1412
msgid ""
"ProcessGroupNCCL implements barrier as an all_reduce of a 1-element "
"tensor. A device must be chosen for allocating this tensor. The device "
"choice is made by checking in this order (1) the first device passed to "
"device_ids arg of barrier if not None, (2) the device passed to "
"init_process_group if not None, (3) the device that was first used with "
"this process group, if another collective with tensor inputs has been "
"performed, (4) the device index indicated by the global rank mod local "
"device count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1414
msgid ""
"Synchronize processes similar to torch.distributed.barrier, but consider "
"a configurable timeout."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1416
msgid ""
"It is able to report ranks that did not pass this barrier within the "
"provided timeout. Specifically, for non-zero ranks, will block until a "
"send/recv is processed from rank 0. Rank 0 will block until all send "
"/recv from other ranks are processed, and will report failures for ranks "
"that failed to respond in time. Note that if one rank does not reach the "
"monitored_barrier (for example due to a hang), all other ranks would fail"
" in monitored_barrier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1418
msgid ""
"This collective will block all processes/ranks in the group, until the "
"whole group exits the function successfully, making it useful for "
"debugging and synchronizing. However, it can have a performance impact "
"and should only be used for debugging or scenarios that require full "
"synchronization points on the host-side. For debugging purposes, this "
"barrier can be inserted before the application’s collective calls to "
"check if any ranks are desynchronized."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1420
msgid "Note that this collective is only supported with the GLOO backend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1424
msgid ""
"timeout (datetime.timedelta, optional) – Timeout for monitored_barrier. "
"If None, the default process group timeout will be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1426
msgid ""
"wait_all_ranks (bool, optional) – Whether to collect all failed ranks or "
"not. By default, this is False and monitored_barrier on rank 0 will throw"
" on the first failed rank it encounters in order to fail fast. By setting"
" wait_all_ranks=True monitored_barrier will collect all failed ranks and "
"throw an error containing information about all failed ranks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1428
msgid ""
"A Work object represents the handle to a pending asynchronous operation "
"in PyTorch’s distributed package. It is returned by non-blocking "
"collective operations, such as dist.all_reduce(tensor, async_op=True)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1430
msgid ""
"Blocks the currently active GPU stream on the operation to complete. For "
"GPU based collectives this is equivalent to synchronize. For CPU "
"initiated collectives such as with Gloo this will block the CUDA stream "
"until the operation is complete."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1432
msgid "This returns immediately in all cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1434
msgid ""
"To check whether an operation was successful you should check the Work "
"object result asynchronously."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1436
msgid ""
"A torch.futures.Future object which is associated with the completion of "
"the Work. As an example, a future object can be retrieved by fut = "
"process_group.allreduce(tensors).get_future()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1438
msgid ""
"Below is an example of a simple allreduce DDP communication hook that "
"uses get_future API to retrieve a Future associated with the completion "
"of allreduce."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1440 ../../source/references_other.md:1953
msgid ""
"get_future API supports NCCL, and partially GLOO and MPI backends (no "
"support for peer-to-peer operations like send/recv) and will return a "
"torch.futures.Future."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1442
msgid ""
"In the example above, allreduce work will be done on GPU using NCCL "
"backend, fut.wait() will return after synchronizing the appropriate NCCL "
"streams with PyTorch’s current device streams to ensure we can have "
"asynchronous CUDA execution and it does not wait for the entire operation"
" to complete on GPU. Note that CUDAFuture does not support "
"TORCH_NCCL_BLOCKING_WAIT flag or NCCL’s barrier(). In addition, if a "
"callback function was added by fut.then(), it will wait until WorkNCCL’s "
"NCCL streams synchronize with ProcessGroupNCCL’s dedicated callback "
"stream and invoke the callback inline after running the callback on the "
"callback stream. fut.then() will return another CUDAFuture that holds the"
" return value of the callback and a CUDAEvent that recorded the callback "
"stream."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1444
msgid ""
"For CPU work, fut.done() returns true when work has been completed and "
"value() tensors are ready."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1446
msgid ""
"For GPU work, fut.done() returns true only whether the operation has been"
" enqueued."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1448
msgid ""
"For mixed CPU-GPU work (e.g. sending GPU tensors with GLOO), fut.done() "
"returns true when tensors have arrived on respective nodes, but not yet "
"necessarily synched on respective GPUs (similarly to GPU work)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1450
msgid ""
"A torch.futures.Future object of int type which maps to the enum type of "
"WorkResult As an example, a future object can be retrieved by fut = "
"process_group.allreduce(tensor).get_future_result()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1452
msgid ""
"users can use fut.wait() to blocking wait for the completion of the work "
"and get the WorkResult by fut.value(). Also, users can use "
"fut.then(call_back_func) to register a callback function to be called "
"when the work is completed, without blocking the current thread."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1454
msgid "get_future_result API supports NCCL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1456
msgid ""
"In normal cases, users do not need to set the timeout. calling wait() is "
"the same as calling synchronize(): Letting the current stream block on "
"the completion of the NCCL work. However, if timeout is set, it will "
"block the CPU thread until the NCCL work is completed or timed out. If "
"timeout, exception will be thrown."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1458
msgid ""
"An enum-like class for available reduction operations: SUM, PRODUCT, MIN,"
" MAX, BAND, BOR, BXOR, and PREMUL_SUM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1460
msgid ""
"BAND, BOR, and BXOR reductions are not available when using the NCCL "
"backend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1462
msgid ""
"AVG divides values by the world size before summing across ranks. AVG is "
"only available with the NCCL backend, and only for NCCL versions 2.10 or "
"later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1464
msgid ""
"PREMUL_SUM multiplies inputs by a given scalar locally before reduction. "
"PREMUL_SUM is only available with the NCCL backend, and only available "
"for NCCL versions 2.11 or later. Users are supposed to use "
"torch.distributed._make_nccl_premul_sum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1466
msgid "Additionally, MAX, MIN and PRODUCT are not supported for complex tensors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1468
msgid ""
"The values of this class can be accessed as attributes, e.g., "
"ReduceOp.SUM. They are used in specifying strategies for reduction "
"collectives, e.g., reduce()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1470
msgid "This class does not support __members__ property."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1472
msgid ""
"Deprecated enum-like class for reduction operations: SUM, PRODUCT, MIN, "
"and MAX."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1474
msgid "ReduceOp is recommended to use instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1476
msgid ""
"The distributed package comes with a distributed key-value store, which "
"can be used to share information between processes in the group as well "
"as to initialize the distributed package in "
"torch.distributed.init_process_group() (by explicitly creating the store "
"as an alternative to specifying init_method.) There are 3 choices for "
"Key-Value Stores: TCPStore, FileStore, and HashStore."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1478
msgid ""
"Base class for all store implementations, such as the 3 provided by "
"PyTorch distributed: (TCPStore, FileStore, and HashStore)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1480
msgid ""
"The first call to add for a given key creates a counter associated with "
"key in the store, initialized to amount. Subsequent calls to add with the"
" same key increment the counter by the specified amount. Calling add() "
"with a key that has already been set in the store by set() will result in"
" an exception."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1482
msgid "key (str) – The key in the store whose counter will be incremented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1484
msgid "amount (int) – The quantity by which the counter will be incremented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1486
msgid ""
"Append the key-value pair into the store based on the supplied key and "
"value. If key does not exists in the store, it will be created."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1488
msgid "key (str) – The key to be appended to the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1490 ../../source/references_other.md:1572
msgid "value (str) – The value associated with key to be added to the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1492
msgid ""
"The call to check whether a given list of keys have value stored in the "
"store. This call immediately returns in normal cases but still suffers "
"from some edge deadlock cases, e.g, calling check after TCPStore has been"
" destroyed. Calling check() with a list of keys that one wants to check "
"whether stored in the store or not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1494
msgid "keys (list[str]) – The keys to query whether stored in the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1496
msgid ""
"Clones the store and returns a new object that points to the same "
"underlying store. The returned store can be used concurrently with the "
"original object. This is intended to provide a safe way to use a store "
"from multiple threads by cloning one store per thread."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1498
msgid ""
"Inserts the key-value pair into the store based on the supplied key and "
"performs comparison between expected_value and desired_value before "
"inserting. desired_value will only be set if expected_value for the key "
"already exists in the store or if expected_value is an empty string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1500
msgid "key (str) – The key to be checked in the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1502
msgid ""
"expected_value (str) – The value associated with key to be checked before"
" insertion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1504
msgid ""
"desired_value (str) – The value associated with key to be added to the "
"store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1506
msgid ""
"Deletes the key-value pair associated with key from the store. Returns "
"true if the key was successfully deleted, and false if it was not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1508
msgid ""
"The delete_key API is only supported by the TCPStore and HashStore. Using"
" this API with the FileStore will result in an exception."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1510
msgid "key (str) – The key to be deleted from the store"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1512
msgid "True if key was deleted, otherwise False."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1514
msgid ""
"Retrieves the value associated with the given key in the store. If key is"
" not present in the store, the function will wait for timeout, which is "
"defined when initializing the store, before throwing an exception."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1516
msgid "key (str) – The function will return the value associated with this key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1518
msgid "Value associated with key if key is in the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1520
msgid "Returns true if the store supports extended operations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1522
msgid ""
"Retrieve all values in keys. If any key in keys is not present in the "
"store, the function will wait for timeout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1524
msgid "keys (List[str]) – The keys to be retrieved from the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1526
msgid ""
"Inserts a list key-value pair into the store based on the supplied keys "
"and values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1528
msgid "keys (List[str]) – The keys to insert."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1530
msgid "values (List[str]) – The values to insert."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1532
msgid ""
"Returns the number of keys set in the store. Note that this number will "
"typically be one greater than the number of keys added by set() and add()"
" since one key is used to coordinate all the workers using the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1534
msgid ""
"When used with the TCPStore, num_keys returns the number of keys written "
"to the underlying file. If the store is destructed and another store is "
"created with the same file, the original keys will be retained."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1536
msgid "The number of keys present in the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1538
msgid "Returns the length of the specified queue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1540
msgid "If the queue doesn’t exist it returns 0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1542 ../../source/references_other.md:1548
msgid "See queue_push for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1544
msgid "key (str) – The key of the queue to get the length."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1546
msgid ""
"Pops a value from the specified queue or waits until timeout if the queue"
" is empty."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1550
msgid ""
"If block is False, a dist.QueueEmptyError will be raised if the queue is "
"empty."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1552
msgid "key (str) – The key of the queue to pop from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1554
msgid "block (bool) – Whether to block waiting for the key or immediately return."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1556
msgid "Pushes a value into the specified queue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1558
msgid ""
"Using the same key for queues and set/get operations may result in "
"unexpected behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1560
msgid "wait/check operations are supported for queues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1562
msgid "wait with queues will only wake one waiting worker rather than all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1564
msgid "key (str) – The key of the queue to push to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1566
msgid "value (str) – The value to push into the queue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1568
msgid ""
"Inserts the key-value pair into the store based on the supplied key and "
"value. If key already exists in the store, it will overwrite the old "
"value with the new supplied value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1570
msgid "key (str) – The key to be added to the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1574
msgid ""
"Sets the store’s default timeout. This timeout is used during "
"initialization and in wait() and get()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1576
msgid "timeout (timedelta) – timeout to be set in the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1578
msgid "Gets the timeout of the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1580
msgid ""
"wait(self: torch._C._distributed_c10d.Store, arg0: "
"collections.abc.Sequence[str]) -> None"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1582
msgid ""
"Waits for each key in keys to be added to the store. If not all keys are "
"set before the timeout (set during store initialization), then wait will "
"throw an exception."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1584 ../../source/references_other.md:1590
msgid ""
"keys (list) – List of keys on which to wait until they are set in the "
"store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1586
msgid ""
"wait(self: torch._C._distributed_c10d.Store, arg0: "
"collections.abc.Sequence[str], arg1: datetime.timedelta) -> None"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1588
msgid ""
"Waits for each key in keys to be added to the store, and throws an "
"exception if the keys have not been set by the supplied timeout."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1592
msgid ""
"timeout (timedelta) – Time to wait for the keys to be added before "
"throwing an exception."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1594
msgid ""
"A TCP-based distributed key-value store implementation. The server store "
"holds the data, while the client stores can connect to the server store "
"over TCP and perform actions such as set() to insert a key-value pair, "
"get() to retrieve a key-value pair, etc. There should always be one "
"server store initialized because the client store(s) will wait for the "
"server to establish a connection."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1596
msgid ""
"host_name (str) – The hostname or IP Address the server store should run "
"on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1598
msgid ""
"port (int) – The port on which the server store should listen for "
"incoming requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1600
msgid ""
"world_size (int, optional) – The total number of store users (number of "
"clients + 1 for the server). Default is None (None indicates a non-fixed "
"number of store users)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1602
msgid ""
"is_master (bool, optional) – True when initializing the server store and "
"False for client stores. Default is False."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1604
msgid ""
"timeout (timedelta, optional) – Timeout used by the store during "
"initialization and for methods such as get() and wait(). Default is "
"timedelta(seconds=300)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1606
msgid ""
"wait_for_workers (bool, optional) – Whether to wait for all the workers "
"to connect with the server store. This is only applicable when world_size"
" is a fixed value. Default is True."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1608
msgid ""
"multi_tenant (bool, optional) – If True, all TCPStore instances in the "
"current process with the same host/port will use the same underlying "
"TCPServer. Default is False."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1610
msgid ""
"master_listen_fd (int, optional) – If specified, the underlying TCPServer"
" will listen on this file descriptor, which must be a socket already "
"bound to port. To bind an ephemeral port we recommend setting the port to"
" 0 and reading .port. Default is None (meaning the server creates a new "
"socket and attempts to bind it to port)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1612
msgid ""
"use_libuv (bool, optional) – If True, use libuv for TCPServer backend. "
"Default is True."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1614
msgid "Creates a new TCPStore."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1616
msgid "Gets the hostname on which the store listens for requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1618
msgid "Returns True if it’s using the libuv backend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1620
msgid "Gets the port number on which the store listens for requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1622
msgid ""
"A thread-safe store implementation based on an underlying hashmap. This "
"store can be used within the same process (for example, by other "
"threads), but cannot be used across processes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1624
msgid "Creates a new HashStore."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1626
msgid ""
"A store implementation that uses a file to store the underlying key-value"
" pairs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1628
msgid "file_name (str) – path of the file in which to store the key-value pairs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1630
msgid ""
"world_size (int, optional) – The total number of processes using the "
"store. Default is -1 (a negative value indicates a non-fixed number of "
"store users)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1632
msgid "Creates a new FileStore."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1634
msgid "Gets the path of the file used by FileStore to store key-value pairs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1636
msgid ""
"A wrapper around any of the 3 key-value stores (TCPStore, FileStore, and "
"HashStore) that adds a prefix to each key inserted to the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1638
msgid ""
"prefix (str) – The prefix string that is prepended to each key before "
"being inserted into the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1640
msgid ""
"store (torch.distributed.store) – A store object that forms the "
"underlying key-value store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1642
msgid "Creates a new PrefixStore."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1644
msgid "Gets the underlying store object that PrefixStore wraps around."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1646
msgid ""
"Note that you can use torch.profiler (recommended, only available after "
"1.8.1) or torch.autograd.profiler to profile collective communication and"
" point-to-point communication APIs mentioned here. All out-of-the-box "
"backends (gloo, nccl, mpi) are supported and collective communication "
"usage will be rendered as expected in profiling output/traces. Profiling "
"your code is the same as any regular torch operator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1648
msgid ""
"Please refer to the profiler documentation for a full overview of "
"profiler features."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1650
msgid ""
"The multi-GPU functions (which stand for multiple GPUs per CPU thread) "
"are deprecated. As of today, PyTorch Distributed’s preferred programming "
"model is one device per thread, as exemplified by the APIs in this "
"document. If you are a backend developer and want to support multiple "
"devices per thread, please contact PyTorch Distributed’s maintainers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1652
msgid ""
"Object collectives have a number of serious limitations. Read further to "
"determine if they are safe to use for your use case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1654
msgid ""
"Object collectives are a set of collective-like operations that work on "
"arbitrary Python objects, as long as they can be pickled. There are "
"various collective patterns implemented (e.g. broadcast, all_gather, …) "
"but they each roughly follow this pattern:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1656
msgid ""
"convert the input object into a pickle (raw bytes), then shove it into a "
"byte tensor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1658
msgid ""
"communicate the size of this byte tensor to peers (first collective "
"operation)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1660
msgid "allocate appropriately sized tensor to perform the real collective"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1662
msgid "communicate the object data (second collective operation)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1664
msgid "convert raw data back into Python (unpickle)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1666
msgid ""
"Object collectives sometimes have surprising performance or memory "
"characteristics that lead to long runtimes or OOMs, and thus they should "
"be used with caution. Here are some common issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1668
msgid ""
"Asymmetric pickle/unpickle time - Pickling objects can be slow, depending"
" on the number, type and size of the objects. When the collective has a "
"fan-in (e.g. gather_object), the receiving rank(s) must unpickle N times "
"more objects than the sending rank(s) had to pickle, which can cause "
"other ranks to time out on their next collective."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1670
msgid ""
"Inefficient tensor communication - Tensors should be sent via regular "
"collective APIs, not object collective APIs. It is possible to send "
"Tensors via object collective APIs, but they will be serialized and "
"deserialized (including a CPU-sync and device-to-host copy in the case of"
" non-CPU tensors), and in almost every case other than debugging or "
"troubleshooting code, it would be worth the trouble to refactor the code "
"to use non-object collectives instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1672
msgid ""
"Unexpected tensor devices - If you still want to send tensors via object "
"collectives, there is another aspect specific to cuda (and possibly other"
" accelerators) tensors. If you pickle a tensor that is currently on "
"cuda:3, and then unpickle it, you will get another tensor on cuda:3 "
"regardless of which process you are on, or which CUDA device is the "
"‘default’ device for that process. With regular tensor collective APIs, "
"‘output tensors’ will always be on the same, local device, which is "
"generally what you’d expect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1674
msgid ""
"Unpickling a tensor will implicitly activate a CUDA context if it is the "
"first time a GPU is used by the process, which can waste significant "
"amounts of GPU memory. This issue can be avoided by moving tensors to CPU"
" before passing them as inputs to an object collective."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1676
msgid ""
"Besides the builtin GLOO/MPI/NCCL backends, PyTorch distributed supports "
"third-party backends through a run-time register mechanism. For "
"references on how to develop a third-party backend through C++ Extension,"
" please refer to Tutorials - Custom C++ and CUDA Extensions and "
"test/cpp_extensions/cpp_c10d_extension.cpp. The capability of third-party"
" backends are decided by their own implementations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1678
msgid ""
"The new backend derives from c10d::ProcessGroup and registers the backend"
" name and the instantiating interface through "
"torch.distributed.Backend.register_backend() when imported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1680
msgid ""
"When manually importing this backend and invoking "
"torch.distributed.init_process_group() with the corresponding backend "
"name, the torch.distributed package runs on the new backend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1682
msgid "The support of third-party backend is experimental and subject to change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1684
msgid ""
"The torch.distributed package also provides a launch utility in "
"torch.distributed.launch. This helper utility can be used to launch "
"multiple processes per node for distributed training."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1686
msgid "Module torch.distributed.launch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1688
msgid ""
"torch.distributed.launch is a module that spawns up multiple distributed "
"training processes on each of the training nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1690
msgid "This module is going to be deprecated in favor of torchrun."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1692
msgid ""
"The utility can be used for single-node distributed training, in which "
"one or more processes per node will be spawned. The utility can be used "
"for either CPU training or GPU training. If the utility is used for GPU "
"training, each distributed process will be operating on a single GPU. "
"This can achieve well-improved single-node training performance. It can "
"also be used in multi-node distributed training, by spawning up multiple "
"processes on each node for well-improved multi-node distributed training "
"performance as well. This will especially be beneficial for systems with "
"multiple Infiniband interfaces that have direct-GPU support, since all of"
" them can be utilized for aggregated communication bandwidth."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_other.md:1694
msgid ""
"In both cases of single-node distributed training or multi-node "
"distributed training, this utility will launch the given number of "
"processes per node (--nproc-per-node). If used for GPU training, this "
"number needs to be less or equal to the number of GPUs on the current "
"system (nproc_per_node), and each process will be operating on a single "
"GPU from GPU 0 to GPU (nproc_per_node - 1)."
msgstr ""

